"use strict";(self.webpackChunkwhaleal_platform=self.webpackChunkwhaleal_platform||[]).push([[98],{4496:function(e,t,a){a.d(t,{o:function(){return s}});const s=[{slug:"byol",title:"Bring your own account",subtitle:"What changes when the provider account stays yours",excerpt:"The platform has no provider account of its own. That one design decision changes who owns the numbers, who holds the quota, and who appears on the invoice.",readMinutes:4,updated:"2026-10-12",sections:[{h:"The model in one paragraph",p:["You keep your account with Twilio, Vonage, Infobip, MessageBird, Plivo or AWS. You give the platform credentials with permission to send. When a message goes out, the platform calls that provider using your credentials, from your account, against your quota, and the provider bills you at the rate you negotiated with them.","The platform never holds a provider account, never resells message volume, and never appears on the invoice you get from your provider. What you pay the platform is a flat subscription for the orchestration layer and nothing else."]},{h:"What this actually buys you",list:["Your sender numbers stay yours. If you stop using the platform, the numbers, registrations and history with the provider do not move.","Your negotiated rate stays your negotiated rate. There is no markup applied on top of it, because there is no message margin to apply it to.","Your relationship with the provider stays direct. When they change a limit, run a maintenance window or need to verify your account, you deal with them as their customer, not through us."]},{h:"What it does not buy you",list:["It does not transfer your provider account, approvals or credit line. Those remain yours to keep in good standing.","It does not make your quota larger. Your provider grants you a rate; the platform can only queue to it, never raise it.","It does not put us between you and your provider in a dispute. We cannot escalate on your behalf, because we are not their customer."]},{h:"Why a flat subscription is the honest shape",p:["If message volume is billed by your provider and not by us, then a per-message price on our side would be a margin on somebody else’s bill, with no corresponding cost of ours. A flat subscription keyed to the things we actually operate — how many connections you run, how fast you may submit, how long logs are kept — is the shape that matches where the work is."]}]},{slug:"sms-segments",title:"SMS segments and what you actually pay for",subtitle:"Why the same text is sometimes one billable message and sometimes three",excerpt:"SMS is billed per segment, not per message. Whether your text fits in one segment depends on the character set it needs — and on the encoding you set on the connection.",readMinutes:5,updated:"2026-10-12",sections:[{h:"Two character sets, two capacities",p:["The GSM-7 alphabet covers the letters, digits and punctuation needed for most Western European languages. Anything outside it — Chinese, Cyrillic, Arabic, emoji, curly quotes — forces the message into UCS-2, which has far less room per segment."],table:{head:["Encoding","Single segment","Per segment in a long message"],rows:[["GSM-7","160 characters","153 characters"],["UCS-2","70 characters","67 characters"],["Byte-counted gateway (GBK / UTF-8)","140 bytes","134 bytes"]]},p2:["The per-segment figure is lower than the single figure because a long message is split across several parts, and each part carries a small header so the handset can reassemble them in order. That header is charged as part of the segment."]},{h:"A trailing detail that surprises people",p:["A handful of characters — the euro sign, curly braces, backslash, tilde, caret — live in the GSM-7 extended table. They are representable in GSM-7, but each one occupies two septets instead of one, because it is sent as an escape sequence. A single one of them can be the character that pushes a 160-character message into a second segment."]},{h:"What the platform does with this",p:["Segments are computed before the message is handed to the provider, and the count is kept on the record. That is the number your provider will bill you for, so you can reconcile the two without guessing.","Each connection carries an encoding setting. On the default, the platform inspects the text and picks GSM-7 if every character fits, otherwise UCS-2 — which is what the major providers do on their own side. You can also pin it: forcing GSM-7 makes a message containing a non-GSM-7 character fail outright rather than silently being transmitted as question marks or silently billed at double the segments."],scope:"Segment accounting applies to the SMS channel. It is a property of how SMS is transmitted and billed, and has no equivalent on the email or WhatsApp channels."},{h:"The practical rule",list:["Keep marketing copy inside GSM-7 where you can — one stray curly quote can double a long message.","If a message must go out in a non-Latin script, budget roughly 70 characters per segment at the single-segment rate.","Check the segment count on the record before you compare it against a provider invoice; that is where a mismatch will show up first."]}]},{slug:"opt-out",title:"Opt-out handling",subtitle:"What happens after a recipient replies STOP",excerpt:"Opt-out is two halves: the suppression record, and the check that runs before a marketing message is handed to the provider. Both run through one shared normalization, which is the part that usually breaks.",readMinutes:7,updated:"2026-10-12",sections:[{h:"The two halves",p:["A suppression record is written when a recipient asks to stop. The record belongs to a tenant, a channel and an address, and it is written idempotently: a repeated opt-out refreshes the existing record rather than creating a second one.","The check runs before a marketing message is handed to the provider. If the address is suppressed, the message is not sent and the outcome is recorded as suppressed rather than as a failure — it was never attempted, so counting it as a delivery failure would be wrong."]},{h:"Which replies count as an opt-out",p:["The recognition list follows the North American carrier standard: STOP, STOPALL, UNSUBSCRIBE, CANCEL, END and QUIT. OPTOUT and OPT OUT are included as widely supported variants. Because the messages the platform sends are not always in English, the local-language equivalent that appears in its own opt-out footer is recognized as well — so a recipient who replies in the language they were written to is not left subscribed."]},{h:"The matching is exact, and that is deliberate",p:["Before matching, the reply is normalized: case is folded and punctuation is collapsed to spaces. Matching then requires the whole normalized reply to equal a keyword. It is not a substring search.",'Substring matching would classify a reply like "please do not stop the promotion" as an opt-out, silently ending the conversation with a customer who asked for the opposite. The cost of exact matching is that an unusual phrasing such as "STOP please" is not auto-recognized — that one goes to a human, and the console can add it to the suppression list by hand.']},{h:"Coming back: START, YES and UNSTOP",p:["Opt-out is required to be reversible. START, YES and UNSTOP are treated as the reverse action: the suppression record for that address is deactivated and a confirmation is sent back.","The confirmation goes out as transactional rather than marketing traffic. That ordering matters: if the recipient is still suppressed at the moment the confirmation is composed, a marketing classification would be blocked by the very gate it is confirming."]},{h:"HELP is a separate branch, and it is always answered",p:["Replies that ask for help are answered with an explanation, and that reply is sent even to an address that has already opted out. A recipient who asked to stop and then asked for help is still entitled to the help text."]},{h:"Two limits you should know about",list:["Some provider-side opt-out handling is carrier-level. A reply to STOP may be handled by the carrier and never delivered to the platform as an inbound message at all. Where that is the case, the platform recognizes the traffic pattern and logs it distinctly rather than pretending it has handled something it never saw.","Deactivating a record is a database change, and it is not the same as being deliverable again. On some sender types the carrier may have blocked the number on its own side; restoring the record does not undo that, and the carrier has to release it."]},{h:"One normalization, used by both sides",p:["The write path and the read path share the same address normalization. This is the detail that decides whether the feature works at all: if the record is written under one spelling of a number and the check queries another, the gate silently stops matching, nothing errors and nothing turns red, and the recipient keeps receiving messages they opted out of."],scope:"Suppression is implemented for the SMS channel and is the only channel where it is enforced today. Do not assume it carries across to email or WhatsApp."}]},{slug:"delivery-receipts",title:"Reading a delivery receipt",subtitle:"What a status actually tells you, and what it does not",excerpt:"A receipt is a sequence of states, not a verdict. Treating an intermediate one as final is the most common way a sending dashboard ends up lying to you.",readMinutes:5,updated:"2026-10-12",sections:[{h:"Acceptance is not delivery",p:["When a provider accepts a message, all it has confirmed is that the request was well-formed and the provider has taken responsibility for it. The handset has not been reached. Delivery happens later, asynchronously, and arrives as a separate event.","These are different facts and they belong in different columns. A dashboard that shows accepted messages as delivered is not being optimistic; it is reporting something that has not happened yet."]},{h:"Receipts arrive in stages",p:["A message commonly passes through a queued state, a sent or submitted state, and finally a delivered or failed state. Some providers also emit states that are only meaningful while a message is being retried.","The intermediate states are informational. Only the terminal outcome should be used to decide whether a message arrived, and a system that promotes an intermediate state to a terminal one will produce numbers that are wrong in the direction of looking better than reality."]},{h:"Normalized, with the original kept",p:["Each provider reports statuses in its own vocabulary. The platform maps them into one shared set so that the same outcome reads the same way regardless of which provider handled the message, and keeps the raw provider code alongside so nothing is lost in the translation.","The practical effect is that a failure reason is comparable across providers, while a provider-specific error still has a code you can hand to that provider support team verbatim."]},{h:"Forwards, not just views",p:["Receipts are not only a dashboard concern. The platform can forward them to an endpoint you control, so delivery outcomes can drive your own systems — a retry, a support ticket, a customer notification."],scope:"Delivery receipt collection and forwarding are implemented for the SMS channel. Email and WhatsApp receipts are not implemented for those channels today."}]},{slug:"pacing",title:"Pacing, quotas and why we queue",subtitle:"Sending slower on purpose, to send more overall",excerpt:"Every connection carries a send rate. The platform queues to that rate instead of firing as fast as a provider will accept, because the fastest way to get throttled is to send at the speed that causes it.",readMinutes:5,updated:"2026-10-12",sections:[{h:"What the setting means",p:["A connection has a send rate, expressed as messages per second. When a batch is running, the platform releases messages to the provider at that rate rather than submitting everything at once.","The default per provider is deliberately conservative. It is a starting point, not a statement about your account — your real ceiling is set by your provider, based on your account level, the number type and the destination country."]},{h:"Where the limit actually lives",p:["This is the part worth getting right: providers generally rate-limit per sending number, not per account or per connection. If a connection has several sender numbers bound to it, the effective capacity is roughly the per-number rate multiplied by the number of numbers.","So the value on a connection has to be set with the number count in mind. Leaving it at the default when you have bound five numbers is not cautious, it is slow for no reason."]},{h:"Throttling is not a failure",p:["When a provider pushes back because a request arrived too fast, that is a pacing signal, not a broken send. The platform backs off and retries the message rather than marking it failed and moving on. Marking a throttled message as failed would produce a retry storm against an already-congested provider, which is exactly the situation that escalated the throttling in the first place."]},{h:"Two rates, and the lower one wins",p:["There are two separate rates in play. The subscription tier sets a submit rate — how fast you may hand messages to the platform. The connection sets a send rate — how fast the platform calls your provider.","Whichever is lower governs. Raising the plan submit rate does not raise your provider quota, and raising the connection rate does not bypass the plan."]},{h:"What is on your side",list:["Knowing your actual provider quota, including whether it is per number or per account.","Any destination-specific registration your provider requires before it will carry traffic to that country.","Choosing how many sender numbers to bind to a connection, which is what actually sets the ceiling."]}]},{slug:"routing-failover",title:"Routing and failover",subtitle:"What happens when the primary provider has a bad hour",excerpt:"Routes are ordered by priority and weighted within a connection. The point of the arrangement is that a provider rejecting sends degrades into slower sending, not into silence.",readMinutes:4,updated:"2026-10-12",sections:[{h:"Priority decides who goes first",p:["Connections on a route carry a priority. The highest-priority connection that is healthy and still inside its pacing budget takes the message. Only when it cannot do so does the next one in line get it.","This is the arrangement that makes a second provider worth configuring. A single connection with no fallback has exactly one failure mode, and it is total."]},{h:"Weight spreads load within a connection",p:["Several sender numbers can be bound to one connection, each with a weight. Messages are distributed across them by that weight, so a number carrying a heavier share takes proportionally more traffic.","Because provider limits are typically per number, spreading across numbers within a connection is often the simplest way to raise real throughput without changing providers."]},{h:"Failure is detected, not assumed",p:["A connection that keeps rejecting sends is taken out of rotation rather than being retried indefinitely. Without that, one misconfigured connection at the top of a route becomes a single point of failure for every message behind it — the position where a mistake costs the most."]},{h:"What failover does not promise",list:["It does not guarantee delivery. It changes which provider carries a message, not whether the destination carrier accepts it.","It does not repair a bad route. If every connection on the route has the same misconfiguration, failover has nowhere to go.","It does not cover email or WhatsApp. Automatic failover is implemented for the SMS channel."]}]},{slug:"message-type-and-consent",title:"Who decides whether a message is marketing",subtitle:"The message type is a declaration, not a guess",excerpt:"The platform asks you to declare what kind of message you are sending instead of inferring it from the text. That one field decides whether the opt-out gate applies — and the honesty of the declaration is yours.",readMinutes:4,updated:"2026-10-13",sections:[{h:"One field, one gate",p:["A send request carries a message type: MARKETING, INFORMATIONAL or TRANSACTIONAL. Exactly one of those three passes through the opt-out gate, and it is MARKETING. Order confirmations, one-time codes and password resets are not blocked by a recipient’s earlier STOP, because a person who opted out of promotions still needs their login to work.","The gate is deliberately narrow. It blocks a single category, and it blocks it before the provider is ever called — so a suppressed message never reaches the carrier and is not billed."]},{h:"Why the platform does not read your message",p:["The platform does not analyze message bodies. It cannot: bodies are not stored, and the gate runs before anything is handed to a provider. That means the declaration is the only input, and a promotional message declared as TRANSACTIONAL will pass.","Passing is not the same as being permitted. In jurisdictions such as the US under the TCPA, a reminder that carries promotional content is still marketing in law, and sending it to someone who opted out is a violation — regardless of the type you declared."]},{h:"Why the default is the strict one",list:["Omitting the field means MARKETING, the most restrictive value. If you forget it, you over-block; you never under-block.","An unrecognised value is rejected with an error rather than silently downgraded. A typo should surface as a broken integration, not as verification codes that quietly stop arriving.","Marketing and transactional traffic should be split on your side, not merged and re-labeled per send."],scope:"The opt-out gate is implemented for the SMS channel. Email and WhatsApp connect on the same model but do not carry this gate today."}]},{slug:"no-idempotency",title:"A timeout is not a failure",subtitle:"Why resending an uncertain request costs you a second message",excerpt:"Sending is accepted asynchronously and there is no de-duplication key. Retrying a request you are unsure about sends a second message and bills for it.",readMinutes:4,updated:"2026-10-13",sections:[{h:"Accepted is not delivered",p:["A successful send response means the platform has taken responsibility for the message and handed it to a provider. It does not mean the message has arrived, or even that it has left. Delivery is a later, separate event that arrives as a receipt.","This matters at the moment your client gives up waiting. By the time a socket times out, the request may already have been accepted and the message already sent."]},{h:"There is no retry safety net",p:["The platform does not offer an idempotency key and does not de-duplicate requests. Sending the same request twice sends two messages and bills for both. That is a deliberate position rather than an omission: a de-duplication window would have to guess how long a caller might retry, and guessing wrong is worse than being explicit."]},{h:"What to do when a request times out",list:["If you received a message ID, query its status or wait for the callback. Do not resend.","If you did not, look the recipient up in the message log by time window and number before doing anything. Do not resend.","A client-side timeout does not roll back a message the platform has already submitted, and a duplicate is billed like any other."]},{h:"Designing around it on your side",p:['The reliable pattern is to generate your own key before you call, store it with the outcome, and treat "no answer" as unknown rather than as failure. Reconcile from the message log or from `GET /open/v1/usage` instead of from your own optimistic counter.'],scope:"Request handling described here is the SMS send path. Email and WhatsApp are accepted through the console today."}]},{slug:"inbound-messages",title:"Two endpoints that look alike and are not",subtitle:"Receipts and replies arrive at separate URLs",excerpt:"Delivery receipts and inbound replies are different endpoints, configured in different places in your provider’s console. Configure only the receipt URL and opt-out handling stops — silently, with no error anywhere.",readMinutes:4,updated:"2026-10-13",sections:[{h:"One endpoint per direction",p:["Outbound outcomes arrive at the receipt URL. Replies from recipients — including STOP — arrive at the inbound URL. They are separate paths and, in most provider consoles, separate configuration screens under different headings.","This is the single most common way to lose opt-out handling. Receipts keep working, the message log looks healthy, and the suppression list simply never receives any entries. Nothing reports a fault, because from the provider’s point of view nothing is wrong."],table:{head:["Direction","Path","Configured under"],rows:[["Outbound outcome","/webhook/sms/{provider}","Delivery receipts / DLR"],["Inbound reply","/webhook/sms/{provider}/inbound","Inbound messages"]]}},{h:"What the token proves, and what it does not",p:["The token in the URL identifies which account the event belongs to. It does not prove that the event genuinely came from the provider — per-provider signature verification is not implemented. Treat the inbound endpoint as authenticated by obscurity of the token, and treat provider signatures as something to verify yourself if your risk model needs it."]},{h:"Not every provider sends STOP to you",list:["Some providers handle opt-out at the carrier level, in which case no inbound event is produced at all and the platform never sees the reply.","Where that happens, your own suppression record is a reconciliation tool rather than the only line of defense.","The provider identifier in the path is case-insensitive, so a differently-cased value is not a configuration bug."],scope:"Inbound handling and suppression are implemented for the SMS channel. Email and WhatsApp do not carry them today."}]},{slug:"resubscribe-and-audit",title:"Getting a number back",subtitle:"Opt-out and opt-in are two directions of the same record",excerpt:"A recipient who opted out can opt back in. The platform deactivates the suppression record rather than deleting it, so the consent trail survives.",readMinutes:3,updated:"2026-10-13",sections:[{h:"The reverse action exists on purpose",p:["Replying START, YES or UNSTOP removes the number from the active suppression set and sends a confirmation. Without this, a single STOP would be permanent with no path back — which is why opt-out and opt-in are required to be symmetric in the first place.","The command is matched as a whole message, not as a substring, so a message that merely contains the word does not trigger it."]},{h:"Deactivated, not deleted",p:['Resubscribing clears the active state and records when and by whom it was done. The original opt-out stays in the record. That is what makes the consent trail auditable: you can answer "was this person suppressed on the day we sent to them?" long after the fact.',"The same capability exists in the console, where a manual release is attributed to the operator who performed it."]},{h:"What the platform cannot undo",p:['Releasing a number affects the messages we send. It does not affect a block applied on the carrier side — with toll-free numbers in particular, the number may have been blocked upstream, and the recipient has to release that themselves. A record that reads "active" is not proof that the handset will accept a message.'],scope:"Suppression and resubscription are implemented for the SMS channel."}]},{slug:"callback-reliability",title:"What happens when your callback is down",subtitle:"Retries, ordering and the one state that never calls back",excerpt:"Terminal outcomes are pushed to a URL you control, with a small bounded retry. Your handler has to tolerate a repeat, and it should not expect a call for every message.",readMinutes:4,updated:"2026-10-13",sections:[{h:"A short, bounded retry",p:["Any non-2xx response or a timeout triggers a retry after roughly one second, then five, then fifteen. Three attempts, then the platform gives up and records the callback as failed. Bounded retries are the honest shape: an endpoint that is down for an hour is not going to be fixed by a queue that keeps hammering it.","The practical consequence is that a callback you miss during a deploy is missed permanently. Reconcile from the message log rather than expecting a later delivery."]},{h:"Assume you may see it twice",p:["A retry can arrive after a response that was slow rather than lost, so duplicates are possible. De-duplicate on the message ID on your side. The platform sends the request with a callback token header, which your handler should verify before trusting the payload."]},{h:"Not every message produces a callback",list:["Only terminal outcomes are pushed. A message that is still in flight is not reported by callback.","A suppressed message never reaches a provider, so it has no delivery outcome to report — its final state is decided at acceptance time.",'Callback delivery is asynchronous: a message can be delivered and its callback still in flight, so treat "no callback yet" as unknown rather than as failure.'],scope:"Delivery receipt collection and forwarding are implemented for the SMS channel."}]},{slug:"two-rate-limits",title:"Which rate limit you just hit",subtitle:"A plan limit and an edge limit look almost identical",excerpt:"There are two throttles in front of a send: the one your plan sets, and a much higher one at the network edge. Both answer with the same status code, so the message text is what tells them apart.",readMinutes:3,updated:"2026-10-13",sections:[{h:"Two layers, two purposes",p:["The plan limit is a product limit: it varies by subscription tier and is the number you can reason about and budget against. The edge limit sits in front of everything and exists only to keep anomalous traffic away from the service — it is set far above any plan ceiling, so a well-behaved integration should never see it."]},{h:"Telling them apart",p:['Both return the same throttling status, and both carry the same business code. What differs is the message text: the edge limit identifies itself with a distinct prefix. Without that distinction you cannot tell "you are sending faster than your plan allows" from "something upstream flagged this traffic", and the two call for different responses.']},{h:"Retrying is not the same as fixing",list:["Throttling is a pacing signal, not a failed message. Backing off and retrying is correct behavior.","Retrying faster never helps: the limit is enforced on a one-second fixed window, so a burst inside the same window simply fails again.","Sending, bulk sending, scheduled sending and the API all draw on the same allowance — they are not separate buckets."]}]},{slug:"sender-selection",title:"Who the recipient actually sees",subtitle:"Automatic sender injection and when to override it",excerpt:"If you do not name a sender, the platform picks one from the connection that carries the message. Naming one explicitly takes priority everywhere.",readMinutes:3,updated:"2026-10-13",sections:[{h:"The default is to choose for you",p:["A connection can have several sender numbers bound to it, each with a weight. When a request does not specify a sender, the platform injects one from the connection that ends up carrying the message, distributing across the bound numbers by weight. The caller does not have to know which number went out.","That rotation is what keeps several numbers useful: provider limits are usually per number, so spreading one connection across its numbers raises real throughput without changing anything upstream."]},{h:"Explicit wins",p:["A sender named on the request takes priority over automatic selection. Use it when a specific number carries meaning — a support line, a national sender ID, or a number the recipient will recognize and reply to."]},{h:"Where this touches opt-out",list:["Suppression is recorded per recipient, not per sender, so switching numbers does not route around an opt-out.","A reply goes back to the number the recipient last saw, so an inconsistent sender set makes replies harder to reason about.","Automatic rotation means the sender is decided per message, not per campaign — read the message record if you need to know which one went out."],scope:"Sender selection and suppression are SMS-channel behavior."}]},{slug:"data-minimisation",title:"What the platform does not keep",subtitle:"No message bodies, and why two fields still matter",excerpt:"Message content is not archived. What is kept is metadata — and among that metadata two fields are load-bearing, because without them a bill could not be reconciled.",readMinutes:3,updated:"2026-10-13",sections:[{h:"Content is not archived",p:["The platform does not persist message bodies. A send is a pass-through: the text goes to the provider, and what comes back is recorded. This is a compliance position rather than a storage saving — holding message content creates obligations the service is not designed to carry.","The direct consequence is that the platform cannot re-derive anything from the text later. Keep your own record on the business side."]},{h:"Two fields you should actually read",p:["Because the body is gone, the encoding that was used and the number of billable segments cannot be recomputed after the fact. Both are returned on the send response and kept on the record — which makes them the ground truth for reconciliation.","The split between how many requests were submitted and how many segments were billed is also deliberate. One long message is a single request and several billable segments, so a month where request count and billed count differ is normal, not a discrepancy."]},{h:"Retention follows the plan",table:{head:["Tier","Log retention"],rows:[["Free","7 days"],["Growth","7 days"],["Pro","30 days"],["Ultra","90 days"]]},scope:"Retention and the metadata-only log apply to every channel — the same window covers all three."}]}]}}]);
//# sourceMappingURL=e7ee0b476f9820a12cbd6b4b01ad2f121a7b2220-f73446f924e0ae6f0ff9.js.map